A Thread is the smallest unit of execution inside a program.
A process has its own memory; threads share the same memory.

Analogy:
Think of a restaurant (process). 
Each waiter (thread) serves customers independently but shares the same kitchen (memory).

Multithreading = running multiple tasks at once

Advantages:
Better CPU utilization
Perform background tasks (e.g., downloading, processing)

Disadvantages:
Harder to debug, possible race conditions

==>start() launches a new thread; calling run() directly just runs in the main thread

==>Runnable is preferred because it separates the task (logic) from the thread (execution)

| Method          | Description                                | Example                             |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------- |
| `start()`       | Starts thread (runs `run()` in new thread) | `t1.start();`                       |
| `sleep(ms)`     | Pause thread temporarily                   | `Thread.sleep(1000);`               |
| `join()`        | Wait for a thread to finish                | `t1.join();`                        |
| `getName()`     | Returns thread name                        | `Thread.currentThread().getName();` |
| `setPriority()` | Change priority (1–10)                     | `t1.setPriority(8);`                |


If two threads update shared data, results become unpredictable (called race condition)

synchronized ensures only one thread enters the method at a time.

| Keyword        | Purpose                                                    |
| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| synchronized` | Ensures only one thread accesses a resource at a time      |
| Method level   | Locks the entire method                                    |
| Block level    | Locks only specific code section                           |
| Lock type      | Object lock (`this`) or Class lock (`static synchronized`) |


| Question                                                      | Short Answer                           |
| ------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| What problem does synchronization solve?                      | Race condition / data inconsistency    |
| Can two threads execute synchronized method at once?          | No, they take turns                    |
| What is the difference between synchronized method and block? | Block gives finer control              |
| Can static methods be synchronized?                           | Yes, uses class-level lock             |
| Does synchronization slow performance?                        | Yes, slightly, due to locking overhead |


ExecutorService reuses threads instead of creating new ones each time — efficient for multiple tasks